If python points to a global interpreter and not the one in this .venv at root of project

uv python find --managed-python 3.14

We want the path to CPython 3.14.10 under uv's managed Python directory.

Then, from the project root:
deactivate 2>/dev/null || true
conda deactivate 2>/dev/null || true

Remove only the uv project's generated virtual environment:
rm -rf .venv

Do not remove dllm_env, your source files, uv.lock or pyproject.toml.

Recreate it explicitly:
uv venv --python 3.14 --managed-python .venv

Then synchronize your locked dependencies:
uv sync --locked --python 3.14 --managed-python

This should select the uv-managed Python rather than a system or Conda interpreter.

Verify:
uv run --locked python -c \
'import sys; print(sys.version); print(sys.executable); print(sys.base_prefix)'

